# Copy this file to .env and replace the placeholder values with your own.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database connection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are two mutually exclusive ways to configure the database. Pick one.
# When DATABASE_URL is set it is used and the five variables below are ignored;
# when it is unset the five variables below are used. Nothing else is required,
# so an existing local .env keeps working untouched.
#
# LOCAL (the default, and what this file is set up for) — five discrete
# variables, no TLS, unchanged from the original setup:
#
#   DATABASE_HOST=localhost
#   DATABASE_PORT=5432
#   DATABASE_NAME=oou_attendance
#   DATABASE_USER=your_database_user
#   DATABASE_PASSWORD=your_database_password
#
# CLOUD / production — a single pooled, TLS connection string instead. This is
# the form the Render backend uses with a Neon database. The five discrete
# variables above are then not needed:
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE?sslmode=verify-full
#
# In production this comes from the Neon **pooled** endpoint (the hostname that
# contains `-pooler`). The direct endpoint is used only for migrations — see
# docs/render-neon-deployment.md. Never point the running service at the direct
# endpoint.
#
# TLS: a DATABASE_URL is verified by default (DATABASE_SSL defaults to true, and
# a URL's own sslmode cannot turn that off). Do not use
# sslmode=require/prefer/disable/no-verify — `pg` honours those and they switch
# certificate verification off, so the application refuses them at startup.
# Omit sslmode entirely and let the application apply its own settings, or use
# sslmode=verify-full. Provider certificates signed by a public CA are verified
# against the system trust store with no extra configuration. If a provider
# uses its own private CA, add &sslrootcert=/path/to/ca.pem to the URL so `pg`
# loads it while still verifying the certificate and hostname.
#
# Set DATABASE_SSL=false only for a non-production database with no TLS, for
# example a throwaway container on localhost. It is rejected outright when
# NODE_ENV=production, so a production connection can never lose TLS.
#
# Never commit a real DATABASE_URL or password. It belongs in backend/.env
# locally and in the hosting provider's encrypted environment variables in the
# cloud. The application never logs either form; startup reports only which
# style of configuration is in use.

DATABASE_HOST=localhost
DATABASE_PORT=5432
DATABASE_NAME=oou_attendance
DATABASE_USER=your_database_user
DATABASE_PASSWORD=your_database_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database connection pool
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explicit values rather than the driver's defaults, because the right size
# depends on how many copies of the API can run at once. All are optional; the
# defaults below are what you get with nothing set. An invalid value fails at
# startup rather than being silently ignored. There is no retry loop anywhere:
# a failing query fails, rather than amplifying load on a struggling database.
#
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10                  # default: 10 local, 1 with DATABASE_URL
# DATABASE_POOL_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
# DATABASE_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# DATABASE_POOL_ALLOW_EXIT_ON_IDLE=false # default: false local, true with DATABASE_URL
#
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX is 1 for a DATABASE_URL on purpose. A serverless platform
# runs many short-lived instances at once, each with its own pool, so the number
# of database connections is multiplied by the number of warm instances. Raise
# it only deliberately, against the provider's connection limit, and prefer a
# pooled connection string (PgBouncer and similar) over a larger pool.
#
# DATABASE_POOL_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS stops a request hanging when the database
# is unreachable, so it fails within the platform's execution limit instead of
# timing out there. DATABASE_POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS returns a frozen instance's
# connection to the pool. DATABASE_POOL_ALLOW_EXIT_ON_IDLE lets a process with
# nothing left to do exit instead of being kept alive by an idle connection.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTP server binding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOST and PORT decide which interface the API listens on. Unset or blank means
# the default shown below. An invalid value fails at startup rather than being
# silently ignored.
#
# HOST=127.0.0.1   # default: loopback only
# PORT=5000        # default
#
# Normal local development — leave HOST at its default. The API is reachable at
# http://127.0.0.1:5000 from this machine only, which is also the safe default for
# a cloud deployment.
#
# LAN mode (opt-in) — uncomment to let devices on the same local network reach
# the API directly:
#
# HOST=0.0.0.0     # every interface
# PORT=5000
#
# Set HOST to a single address (for example a wired interface's address) instead
# of 0.0.0.0 to expose the API on that interface only.
#
# The address a device actually opens is the server PC's own network address, for
# example http://192.168.0.244:4173 through the Vite dev server. Read it from the
# machine (ipconfig / ifconfig); it is a property of the network, not of this
# code, and must never be hard-coded in application source.
#
# Setting HOST only makes the API listen on the local network. It does not
# configure Windows routing, Internet Connection Sharing, NAT, port forwarding or
# firewall rules.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session cookie "Secure" attribute
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A browser drops a Secure cookie from a plain-HTTP page, so this defaults to off
# for local development and LAN mode, and on in production. An unrecognised value
# fails at startup rather than being silently ignored.
#
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true    # default: true in production, false elsewhere
#
# Set it to true only for an HTTPS deployment that does not set NODE_ENV=production.
# It is rejected outright when NODE_ENV=production, so production session cookies
# can never lose the attribute. See docs/lan-https-webauthn.md.
#
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WebAuthn / Passkeys (Relying Party configuration).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only WEBAUTHN_RP_ID and WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN are required in production.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME defaults to "OOU Attendance System" if not set.
#
# In development both default to the local dev origin: WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
# and WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:4173.
#
# WebAuthn requires a secure context. Browsers treat http://localhost as secure
# but NOT http://<LAN-IP>, so passkey ceremonies do not run when the app is
# loaded from the PC's LAN address. Serving the LAN over HTTPS with a certificate
# the phone trusts is a DNS and PKI decision, not a configuration one — see
# docs/lan-https-webauthn.md for what it would take. Do not relax the production
# values below to work around that.
#
# In production both are REQUIRED and the application refuses to start without
# them. The browser's canonical origin is the Vercel frontend, NOT the Render
# host, because the browser only ever loads pages from and talks to the Vercel
# origin and reaches the API through the `/api/*` rewrite:
#
#   WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=oou-attendance-system.vercel.app
#   WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://oou-attendance-system.vercel.app
#   WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=OOU Attendance System
#
# Do NOT set the Render hostname as the RP ID or the expected origin. WebAuthn
# binds a credential to the origin the ceremony actually runs in, so a Render
# value would fail verification in the browser no matter how the proxy is
# configured. There is no preview-host workaround: only this one origin works.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Student access: which deployment serves students
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Student access is split between the two deployments. This switch is the whole
# policy, and it is set by an operator - it is not inferred from anything the
# server can observe.
#
#   STUDENT_ACCESS_MODE=cloud   (default when unset) account setup only
#   STUDENT_ACCESS_MODE=edge                    full student surface
#
# cloud - the public deployment (Render). Only account setup is served: a newly
#        registered student can complete course registration and WebAuthn device
#        enrollment. Student sign-in, the student device-login endpoints,
#        remembered-account, device-binding and attendance all answer
#        403 STUDENT_ACCESS_DISABLED. Administrators and lecturers are unaffected
#        and keep working over the Internet.
# edge  - the K12 PC on the campus router's network. The full student surface:
#        students sign in, mark and view attendance, and use every student API
#        normally.
#
# The web-route allowlists for the cloud live in
# src/middleware/studentAccess.ts - one review point for what the public
# deployment serves.
#
# Unset means cloud, deliberately: a deployment that has not been told it is the
# edge must never be the reason students are exposed to the open Internet. An
# unrecognized value fails at startup rather than defaulting.
#
# This is NOT an IP allowlist. The deployed API sits behind proxies, so the client
# address is not a trustworthy signal and nothing in the policy reads it.
#
# This is NOT SYNC_ENABLED. That variable means "run the background sync worker".
# The two are independent on purpose: an edge may sync or not, and the cloud
# provides the feed either way.
#
# This does NOT make the edge private. It decides who the software will serve.
# Keeping the edge off the Internet is still the router's and the firewall's job -
# see docs/lan-mode.md.
#
# Cloud / Render: set cloud explicitly, or leave it unset.
# Local K12 PC:   set edge.
#
# STUDENT_ACCESS_MODE=edge

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloud <-> K12 edge synchronization
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The local K12 PC is a sync CONSUMER: it pulls attendance sessions from the
# cloud's change feed so students on the K12 LAN can see a running session. The
# cloud is the sync PROVIDER and serves that feed.
#
# Both roles come from this one file, because the same build runs on both sides.
# Nothing here is required for normal development or for the test suite - the
# worker stays off unless SYNC_ENABLED is explicitly set.
#
# --- Local K12 PC (consumer) ---------------------------------------------
# SYNC_ENABLED=true
# SYNC_CLOUD_BASE_URL=https://your-render-host.onrender.com
# SYNC_EDGE_ID=k12-pc-01
# SYNC_EDGE_SECRET=<a long random string shared with the cloud>
# SYNC_INTERVAL_MS=15000
# SYNC_BATCH_LIMIT=100
# SYNC_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#
# SYNC_ENABLED - the master switch for the background worker. Unset or false means
# no worker runs, which is the correct setting for ordinary development and for
# every test run. The cloud deployment must never set this: the cloud is the
# provider, and a provider does not also consume itself.
#
# SYNC_CLOUD_BASE_URL - the cloud API's base URL, with no trailing slash. This is
# the PC's normal Internet connection, entirely separate from the K12 Wi-Fi the
# students use. It requires no change to Windows networking, no Internet
# Connection Sharing, and no NAT or firewall rule: the PC already has outbound
# Internet access, so synchronization is an ordinary outbound HTTPS request.
#
# SYNC_EDGE_ID - identifies this edge in its cursor and processed-event records.
# One configured edge is supported today.
#
# SYNC_EDGE_SECRET - the shared secret. It is server-side only: it lives in this
# file and in the cloud's environment, never in the React frontend, never in a
# browser, and never in a URL. It is sent only as an Authorization: Bearer
# header, is never logged, and never appears in any API response.
#
# SYNC_INTERVAL_MS - how long to wait after an idle poll. Default: 15000.
# Failures use their own backoff (2s, 5s, 10s, 30s, then 60s) regardless.
#
# SYNC_BATCH_LIMIT - maximum change events per request. Default: 100, maximum
# 500. The cloud clamps any larger request to the same ceiling.
#
# SYNC_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS - per-request timeout. Default: 10000.
#
# --- Cloud (provider) ----------------------------------------------------
# SYNC_PROVIDER_SECRET_HASH=<64-character SHA-256 hex digest of SYNC_EDGE_SECRET>
#
# SYNC_PROVIDER_SECRET_HASH - the cloud serves the feed at
# GET /api/internal/sync/changes only to callers that present the matching
# secret. The cloud stores ONLY this digest, never the secret itself, so leaking
# the cloud's configuration still does not let an attacker authenticate. It is
# optional: leave it unset and the feed refuses every caller with
# SYNC_NOT_CONFIGURED rather than defaulting to open.
#
# Generate the pair with Node (never reuse a password):
#
#   node -e "const s=require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url');\
#   console.log('SYNC_EDGE_SECRET='+s);\
#   console.log('SYNC_PROVIDER_SECRET_HASH='+\
#   require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(s).digest('hex'))"
#
# Put the secret in the K12 PC's backend/.env and the digest in Render's
# encrypted environment variables. To rotate, generate a new pair and update
# both sides.
#
# See docs/cloud-k12-sync.md for the full model.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION DEPLOYMENT — Render Web Service
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This section is a reference for the Render backend. It is NOT a file to copy
# to production: the real values belong in Render's environment-variable
# settings, which are encrypted at rest and never enter Git.
#
# This .env.example file itself stays a local-development template. Local
# development keeps using the five discrete DATABASE_* variables above and
# needs none of what follows.
#
# Render service settings (set in the Render dashboard, not here):
#
#   Root Directory   /            (the repository root — there is one lockfile)
#   Build Command    npm ci && npm run build --workspace backend
#   Start Command    npm start --workspace backend     -> node dist/index.js
#   Health Check     /api/health
#
# Required environment variables:
#
#   NODE_ENV=production
#   HOST=0.0.0.0
#   PORT=<render-assigned-port>
#   DATABASE_URL=<neon-pooled-connection-string>
#   WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=oou-attendance-system.vercel.app
#   WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://oou-attendance-system.vercel.app
#   WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=OOU Attendance System
#
# NODE_ENV — set it explicitly. It is what enables the production guards:
# production session cookies are forced to carry Secure, and DATABASE_SSL=false
# becomes an outright startup error.
#
# HOST — must be 0.0.0.0 on Render. The application otherwise defaults to
# 127.0.0.1, which is unreachable from outside the machine, so a service left at
# the default would start and then fail every request.
#
# PORT — Render assigns the port and supplies it as PORT; the application reads
# it and binds it. Leave this value to Render. The local default is 5000 and the
# local behaviour is unchanged by deploying.
#
# DATABASE_URL — the Neon **pooled** connection string, with TLS verification on:
#
#   * `sslmode=verify-full` is accepted. Omitting the sslmode parameter
#     entirely is also accepted and is the simplest option.
#   * `sslmode=require`, `prefer`, `disable` and `no-verify` are REFUSED at
#     startup. Neon hands out `sslmode=require` by default, so a pasted Neon
#     string must be edited to `verify-full` (or have sslmode removed) before it
#     is used, otherwise the backend will refuse to boot.
#   * Do NOT set DATABASE_SSL=false in production. It is rejected outright when
#     NODE_ENV=production, so production TLS cannot be switched off. Leave it
#     unset: it defaults to enabled whenever a DATABASE_URL is in use.
#   * Pool sizing defaults to 1 connection per instance for a DATABASE_URL,
#     which suits a single long-running Render instance behind a pooled
#     endpoint. Raise DATABASE_POOL_MAX only deliberately, against Neon's
#     connection limit.
#
# Migrations use a SEPARATE connection: run them with DATABASE_URL set to the
# Neon **direct** endpoint (the hostname without `-pooler`), from a workstation
# or a one-off job, never from the Render start command and never on startup.
#
#   npm run migrate --workspace backend
#
# Migrations are never part of application startup. The start path only loads
# configuration, creates the app, checks the database with a read-only SELECT 1,
# and listens.
#
# NEVER put any of the following in Git, in this file, or in a commit message:
#
#   * a real DATABASE_URL or database password
#   * any Render secret or API token
#
# This file intentionally contains placeholders only. Real values belong in
# Render's environment settings, and locally in backend/.env, which is ignored
# by Git.
#
# Also never run `npm run seed:e2e`, `npm run unseed:e2e` or
# `npm run test:db:prepare` against production. Those scripts exist only for
# the local/E2E test database.
